WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:01.330 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  Porto Rico is still dealing with the  devastating effects of Hurricane Maria  nearly 16 months after the deadly  category 4 storm slammed ashore 3.2  million Americans live in the u.s.  territory in 2017 Puerto Rico had 710  homicides that's more per capita than  any u.s. state last year though the  number dropped to 641 but so far this  year 22 people have been murdered on the  island David beg nose in Dorado with a  look at the island safety challenges  David I've been to Dorado at the resort  where you are so I know you're safe  there but other places not so good not  so good Gale look this is paradise we  know that but there are a lot of real  problems here that people have asked us  to talk about in fact when we plan this  trip for Hamilton I went on social media  and I asked folks what's the number one  story we can dig into for you and what  we heard more than anything else was  crime so we have spent the last five  days focusing on that and talking to key  players some of whom believe there is a  crisis of violence on this island and  videos like the one I'm about to show  you are why a lot of people are afraid  here  this is not a war zone  it's San Juan Puerto Rico last Sunday  gunfire erupted in an area frequented by  tourists near the airport a man is  killed in the middle of the street by a  gunman who shoots him again as the  driver flees the next day two brothers  were shot when a driver pulled up and  opened fire in broad daylight then  Wednesday a gunman walked up to a man in  his car at a gas station in Dorado and  killed him at 8:40 in the morning do you  feel safe walking on the street at night  no Kira  Nieves Pearson walked with us through  the tourist area of condado she used to  live here  it's not even perception it's literally  it's my husband getting held up at  gunpoint it's my mom getting carjacked  you know it's everybody that I love has  been a victim and it's happening here  Douglas left is the top FBI official in  Puerto Rico  he says the island is facing a crisis of  violence what percentage of homicides  here on the island are gang-related  sadly it's it's pretty close to a  hundred percent left told us the motives  behind almost every killing our money  survival and drugs they could send the  entire FBI down here and they would  still be overworked that's just the  situation that we have here you can now  go to a precinct and sometimes you only  find one police officer Colonel Michelle  Fraley was the superintendent of the  Puerto Rico Police Department in 2017  during Hurricane Maria she asked if one  of our armed security guards would  escort her to our interview because she  doesn't go outside after 6:00 p.m.  personally at night I don't leave my  house unless it's an emergency that I  have to go to the hospital that is a  hell of a statement  from the woman who used to be the head  police it is what it is I'm not hearing  anybody say what can specifically be  done to make this better  it's very easy the government has to set  priorities a couple of weeks ago half a  million dollars were taken out of the  salary account from the police  department and move to the fire  department they're robbing Peter to pay  Paul correct who's gonna work in a place  that you're gonna risk your life for  everybody and you don't have no benefits  no money they don't pay extra hours  that's veteran Puerto Rico police  officer Julio Centron Hernandez he's  planning to resign in June but for now  he's using his paid time off to work at  a warehouse in Florida I'm talking about  thousands of co-workers they'd be  resigning and they've been jumping to  United States to find some work decent  work with benefits how many police  officers have left the force in the last  10 years 10,000 10,000 yeah  sergeant Gregorio Matias represents one  of seven police unions here  he says the officers who are still  serving are demoralized overworked and  underpaid just last week a police  officer went into a bank and took a  hostage saying he wanted to send a  message no one was hurt  that's something right all right so the  Puerto Rico Secretary of Public Safety  Hector pescado told us this in writing  he does not believe there is a crisis of  violence he thinks these social media  videos are highlighting sort of a larger  problem that he doesn't think exists and  he says at the end of the day this  bankrupt island is having to cut every  department including the police but Gale  the real issue here is so many police  officers continue to leave the island by  the hundreds  I talked to 10 of them just this week  who left the island and left their  families behind in search of better pay  and benefits oh it's such a beautiful  island with beautiful people but that's  a very grim picture you painted thank  you very much David you can hear more of  David's conversation with Douglas Levin  he's a top FBI official that you just  saw in David's piece on today's CBS this  morning podcast it's available on  Apple's podcast app and all major  podcast platforms quite a report

